ADS-019 — Compliance Model

Document ID: ADS-019
Title: Compliance Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Compliance Model (CCM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Compliance Model establishes the principles, processes, evidence, and governance required to evaluate, demonstrate, and continuously monitor compliance of Canonical Atlas Objects with applicable engineering standards, organizational policies, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Compliance architecture
Compliance assessments
Compliance evidence
Compliance status
Exception management
Continuous compliance
Compliance reporting
Compliance governance

This specification applies to every governed Canonical Atlas Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Compliance is the demonstrable satisfaction of applicable policies, standards, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements through verifiable evidence associated with Canonical Atlas Objects.

Compliance is demonstrated.

It is not assumed.

4. Objectives

The Compliance Model SHALL:

Demonstrate policy adherence.
Support regulatory readiness.
Preserve compliance evidence.
Enable continuous monitoring.
Support audits.
Support certification.
Preserve traceability.
Support federated governance.
5. Compliance Principles

Every Atlas implementation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Evidence-Based

Compliance SHALL be supported by verifiable evidence.

Continuous Assessment

Compliance SHALL be periodically reassessed.

Traceability

Every compliance decision SHALL be traceable.

Objectivity

Compliance evaluations SHALL follow documented rules.

Explainability

Compliance outcomes SHALL identify supporting evidence and failed obligations.

Governance

Compliance SHALL follow approved governance policies.

6. Compliance Sources

Compliance MAY be evaluated against:

Source	Example
Atlas Specifications	ACC, AHS, ADS
Organizational Policies	Internal governance policies
Engineering Standards	ISO, IEEE, BIM, etc.
Regulatory Requirements	National regulations
Contractual Obligations	Client agreements
Project Requirements	Project-specific rules

Multiple sources MAY apply simultaneously.

7. Compliance Assessment

Each compliance assessment SHALL define:

Property	Description
Assessment ID	Unique identifier
Assessment Scope	Objects being assessed
Applicable Requirements	Rules evaluated
Assessment Method	Manual, automated, hybrid
Assessment Date	Evaluation timestamp
Assessor	Responsible authority
Outcome	Compliance status

Assessments SHALL be versioned.

8. Compliance Evidence

Every assessment SHALL preserve evidence including:

Validation results
Decision Records
Canonical Events
Audit logs
Policy evaluations
Engineering artifacts
Approval records
Trust assertions

Evidence SHALL remain immutable.

9. Compliance Status

Atlas defines the following canonical compliance states.

Status	Description
Compliant	All applicable requirements satisfied
Conditionally Compliant	Minor conditions remain
Non-Compliant	One or more mandatory requirements failed
Exempted	Approved exception exists
Pending Assessment	Evaluation not yet completed

Compliance status SHALL be explicitly recorded.

10. Exception Management

Compliance exceptions SHALL define:

Exception ID
Applicable requirement
Justification
Risk assessment
Approval authority
Expiration date
Mitigation actions

Exceptions SHALL be governed Decision Records.

11. Continuous Compliance

Atlas SHALL support continuous compliance monitoring.

Monitoring MAY detect:

Policy violations
Lifecycle inconsistencies
Missing approvals
Security changes
Relationship inconsistencies
Version incompatibilities
Governance violations

Detected issues SHALL generate Canonical Events.

12. Compliance Reporting

Compliance reports SHALL include:

Assessment scope
Applicable requirements
Assessment outcomes
Supporting evidence
Outstanding issues
Exceptions
Recommendations

Reports SHALL be reproducible.

13. Compliance Governance

Every compliance assessment SHALL define:

Assessment authority
Applicable policies
Review authority
Approval authority
Audit requirements
Retention policy

Compliance governance SHALL remain independent of implementation technology.

14. Quality Requirements

Compliance assessments SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Repeatability
Completeness
Traceability
Explainability
Objectivity
Auditability
Timeliness

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

15. Compliance Events

Compliance activities MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples:

Assessment Started
Assessment Completed
Compliance Achieved
Compliance Failed
Exception Approved
Compliance Restored

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A compliance implementation conforms if it:

Performs governed assessments.
Preserves compliance evidence.
Supports continuous monitoring.
Implements exception management.
Produces standardized compliance reports.
Maintains traceability.
Supports audit readiness.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted compliance assessment.
Predictive compliance monitoring.
Cross-enterprise compliance federation.
Regulatory knowledge integration.
Machine-readable engineering regulations.
Autonomous compliance agents.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical compliance semantics.

